WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.456
ORMs provide raw SQL escape hatches because real applications sometimes
need database-specific behavior, complex reporting, performance tuning, or
migration support.

2
00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:12.759
Raw SQL can be used safely, but it must be reviewed carefully.

3
00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:14.573
Values should be parameterized.

4
00:00:14.573 --> 00:00:16.904
Dynamic structure should be allowlisted.

5
00:00:16.904 --> 00:00:22.215
Authorization filters should not disappear just because the code moved out
of a normal query API.

6
00:00:22.215 --> 00:00:25.519
Raw SQL is a power tool, not a shortcut around security rules.

7
00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:29.210
Stored procedures and database functions deserve the same review.

8
00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:37.824
They may centralize useful logic, but they can also build dynamic SQL,
bypass application-level assumptions, or run with broader privileges than
expected.

9
00:00:37.824 --> 00:00:43.783
Teams should know which layer enforces validation, authorization, tenant
boundaries, and audit behavior.

10
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:49.483
Moving logic into the database can be appropriate, but it should not make
security decisions invisible.

11
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:52.527
Migrations and schema changes are security-relevant.

12
00:00:52.527 --> 00:01:03.278
A migration can add a sensitive field, change a default, loosen a
constraint, backfill data incorrectly, remove an index used for isolation,
or alter a relationship that authorization depends on.

13
00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:06.517
Data migrations can touch large amounts of sensitive data.

14
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:15.260
Migration review should consider integrity, confidentiality, rollback
behavior, and whether application code and database schema change together
safely.

15
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.600
Transactions, locking concepts, and async ORM usage also require
discipline.

16
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.097
Related database changes should succeed or fail consistently.

17
00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:28.862
Bulk updates and async workflows can bypass ordinary per-object handling
or change timing assumptions.

18
00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:35.079
Advanced features are not unsafe by definition, but they can bypass
ordinary model and query safety assumptions.

19
00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:41.880
Review should increase as the feature moves closer to database structure,
concurrency, privilege, or broad data movement.
